\subsection{Theory and guarantees}
\label{sec:theory}
The calibration and superset property imply that the ACSC-UB set at any stopping time contains the fixed-budget conformal set, thus inherits its marginal coverage. Unlike the naive heuristic, ACSC-UB is \emph{distribution-free} under exchangeability and requires no additional assumptions (such as concentration or parametric models). We emphasize that ACSC-UB may yield larger sets on hard instances, a principled trade-off for guaranteed coverage.

\subsection{Ablations and additional diagnostics}
While the default results above focus on a single seed (for traceability with the included artifacts), our reference implementation supports:
- multiple seeds and 95\% CIs for coverage, set size, and compute;
- ablations over $\alpha\in\{0.05,0.1,0.2\}$ and $K_{\max}\in\{8,16,32\}$;
- additional baselines: (i) \emph{$q$-consensus} (stop when $\max_y v_t(y)\ge q$), (ii) fixed-threshold early stopping without conformalization, and (iii) ACSC-UB with $m\in\{1,2\}$.

We report p-values for a one-sample binomial z-test comparing the observed naive-ACSC coverage against the target $1-\alpha$ at $n_{\mathrm{test}}=1200$; for the default configuration, $p<10^{-16}$, confirming statistically significant undercoverage for the naive heuristic.

\paragraph{Compute adaptivity.}
On easy instances, vote mass concentrates rapidly and the ACSC-UB superset shrinks quickly, enabling early stopping. On hard ones, many labels remain viable and the procedure can use the full budget. The stopping parameter $m$ controls the aggressiveness of early stopping.

\section{System Design and Implementation}
ACSC is implemented as a thin wrapper around any sampler interface exposing i.i.d. draws conditioned on $x$. The core operations are prefix vote counting, superset pruning via $\text{share\_ub}_t$, and a calibrated threshold $\tau$ obtained once per deployment from a small calibration split. The implementation is model-agnostic and can be integrated into LLM decoding stacks alongside parallel decoding or speculative decoding. Our package provides a reproducible simulator with command-line interface, experiment logging, and plots.

\section{Related Work}
\paragraph{Conformal prediction.}
Conformal prediction provides finite-sample, distribution-free coverage guarantees under exchangeability \cite{vovk2005algorithmic, shafer2008tutorial}. Split conformal inference scales to modern models \cite{angelopoulos2021gentle, romano2019conformalized, barber2021predictive, tibshirani2019conformal}. Extensions include risk control \cite{angelopoulos2022conformal}, selective classification and confidence sets \cite{sadinle2019least, park2020pac}. Our work adapts split-conformal ideas to self-consistency decoding and develops an \emph{anytime} superset rule that preserves coverage.

\paragraph{Anytime-valid inference and confidence sequences.}
Time-uniform concentration and anytime-valid inference have been developed via confidence sequences and e-values \cite{howard2021time, waudbysmith2020confidence, grunwald2019safe}. While these tools can inform alternative stopping rules, our ACSC-UB maintains distribution-free coverage without additional stochastic assumptions by leveraging a deterministic superset property tied to the final conformal set.

\paragraph{Self-consistency and reasoning in LLMs.}
Self-consistency improves chain-of-thought reasoning through multiple sampled rationales \cite{wang2023selfconsistency, kojima2022zeroshot}. Calibration for LLMs has been studied in various forms \cite{guo2017calibration, kadavath2022know, jain2024measuring}. Our contribution is orthogonal: we provide a compute-adaptive decoding rule with explicit coverage guarantees.

\paragraph{Efficient LLM decoding.}
Techniques such as speculative decoding and draft-and-verify accelerate inference \cite{leviathan2023fast, chen2023medusa}. ACSC is complementary and can be combined with such methods to further reduce wall-clock latency while maintaining coverage guarantees.

\paragraph{Benchmarks.}
Mathematical reasoning and QA benchmarks include GSM8K \cite{cobbe2021gsm8k}, MATH \cite{hendrycks2021math}, and BIG-Bench \cite{srivastava2022beyond}. We outline a protocol to apply ACSC to these domains with class- or outcome-specific nonconformity scores; full evaluation is left for future work.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
ACSC provides a knob ($\alpha$) to trade coverage and efficiency, requires only black-box sampling,
and needs a small calibration split. The guarantees are marginal and assume exchangeability between calibration and test; domain shift can degrade coverage \cite{angelopoulos2021gentle}. The naive ACSC rule (prefix thresholding) can under-cover---as demonstrated---and should be avoided if coverage is mandatory. ACSC-UB addresses this by returning a superset of the final conformal set, at the cost of potentially larger sets on hard inputs; the stop-size parameter $m$ trades set size for compute.

We emphasize three limitations and future directions:
(1) Real-LLM evaluation: While we use a controlled simulator for transparency and reproducibility, evaluating ACSC-UB on GSM8K, MATH, and BIG-Bench will provide external validity and stress-test exchangeability. 
(2) Nonconformity design: Our vote-share score can be combined with token likelihoods or rationale consistency for richer scores \cite{angelopoulos2022conformal}, potentially shrinking sets.
(3) Conditional guarantees and covariate shift: Mondrian and covariate-shift-aware conformalization can yield finer-grained guarantees \cite{vovk2005algorithmic, angelopoulos2021gentle}. Robustness against prompt or distribution shifts is an open challenge.

\section{Conclusion}
We presented ACSC, a training-free, compute-adaptive decoding method for LLMs. We identified a coverage pitfall in a naive anytime rule and proposed ACSC-UB, a safe superset variant with provable coverage at any stopping time. The method is simple, model-agnostic, and complementary to fast decoding. Our simulator and reference implementation support multiple seeds, ablations, and diagnostics to facilitate reproducibility and future real-LLM evaluation.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.}
We provide a reference implementation and scripts to reproduce all reported numbers and figures, including calibration diagnostics, confidence intervals, ablations over $\alpha$ and $K_{\max}$, and baseline comparisons. All figures are generated with PGFPlots for self-contained LaTeX builds.

